# Rationale_ARCHITECT_BRIDGE_FACADE

## Decision 001 - Prefab Binder As DataVault Setter

Problem: Designers need to drag prefabs without manually maintaining hashes, while GPU scatter consumers need stable numeric IDs.

Solution: Use H8PrefabRegistry for authoring and H8PrefabRegistryRuntimeBinder to compact active entries into DataVault buffers. FNV-1a hashes are generated at authoring/sync time, not in draw paths.

Rejected Alternatives: runtime prefab-name lookup, manual hash tables, direct concrete references to scatter systems.

Scalability potential: Low/MX350 reads dense packed records and can drop visual flags. Middle reads lore/acoustic links. High enables high-tier visual hashes. Ultra can attach richer presentation consumers without changing gameplay authority.

Hardware Impact: Low-end avoids string hashing and object lookup during rendering. Runtime impact after sync is 0 us for facade authoring because consumers read raw Vault bits.

## Decision 002 - Design Facade Is Setter-Only

Problem: ScriptableObjects are designer-friendly but unsafe as runtime authority and can mutate persistent editor state.

Solution: H8DesignDataFacade sanitizes inspector values and writes packed float values to BridgeDesignFacadeValues. Runtime systems consume byte-offset values from DataVault and typed DataVaultUpdateSignal notifications.

Rejected Alternatives: polling ScriptableObject fields in gameplay, reflection-based binding, Update sync pump.

Scalability potential: Low uses cheap values such as 1D LUT swaps, triangle noise, and dot-product masks. Middle enables fuller texture settings. High enables raymarch/POM/SSS/particle overkill fields. Ultra keeps same packed contract with more expensive consumers.

Hardware Impact: i3/MX350 receives no per-frame facade cost. Sync cost occurs only on edit/boot/manual push.

## Decision 003 - Pack=1 ABI Discipline

Problem: Quest/ARM64/Mac/PC can disagree on implicit struct padding if DTOs cross binary, native, or DataVault boundaries.

Solution: All Bridge DTOs use explicit StructLayout Pack=1 Size=..., and H8BridgeBinaryLayoutVerifier checks size and field offsets before scene load.

Rejected Alternatives: relying on C# default sequential layout or comments without boot verification.

Scalability potential: Same ABI supports Android/Quest, Metal/Mac, Steam Deck, and PC without per-platform DTO forks.

Hardware Impact: boot-only sentinel cost; hot path avoids defensive conversion.

## Decision 004 - Telemetry Ring And Dump Header

Problem: Live tuning and NaN vaccination must leave forensic evidence, not vague crash reports.

Solution: Store old->new deltas in a 300-entry DataVault-owned telemetry ring and write a self-describing binary dump header with magic/version/count/entry size/cursor/capacity/hash.

Rejected Alternatives: text logs only, unbounded log lists, or chat-only reporting.

Scalability potential: Low keeps compact 40 B entries. High/Ultra can decode richer presentation state from hashes without changing ring shape.

Hardware Impact: fixed 12 KB ring plus 32 B header. Dump is fault path only. Runtime profiler proof is pending.

## Decision 005 - Stress Gate For Live Tuning

Problem: Inspector live tuning during frame stress can add avoidable CPU and signal pressure.

Solution: LiveTuningBlockedByStress checks SignalBus registry stress and homeostasis pressure, disabling live tuning above 0.9 unless designer override is active.

Rejected Alternatives: always-on tuning traffic, hidden quality downgrades, and global throttling that ignores explicit design override.

Scalability potential: Low saves CPU under pressure. Middle/High can keep live authoring when stable. Ultra can carry visual overkill fields without touching gameplay truth.

Hardware Impact: one sync-time branch; no steady-state cost.

## Decision 006 - Generated Default Contracts

Problem: If no H8DesignDataFacade asset exists, the generated contract file can be empty while built-in defaults still exist in code.

Solution: H8BridgeContractGenerator emits built-in default constants first, then appends asset-specific constants. The generated file now includes typed offsets and hashes for SubSpeed, AddedMass, LUT, triangle noise, dot-product vision, silt wake, hull dents, raymarch, POM, SSS, particles, and visor salt growth.

Rejected Alternatives: requiring designers to create an asset before contracts exist, or hardcoding typed constants outside the generator.

Scalability potential: Low gets cheap fake controls. Middle gets texture/visual gates. High/Ultra get overkill controls without hot-path strings.

Hardware Impact: editor generation only; 0 us runtime.

## Decision 007 - Final Compile Without Rebuild Loop

Problem: Task 20 requires compile evidence, but the user explicitly rejected repeated rebuild churn.

Solution: Run one targeted runtime build and one editor build. Hecton8.Core.csproj --no-restore validates runtime Bridge sources. Hecton8.Editor.csproj validates editor windows and generator; it required restore because its assets file was absent, then exited 0.

Rejected Alternatives: full solution rebuild loop, skipping compile evidence, or claiming Unity runtime verification from static scans.

Scalability potential: keeps compile validation focused on Bridge-owned assemblies while avoiding unnecessary broad solution churn.

Hardware Impact: no runtime impact; build-time only.

## Verification Boundary

Unity Editor import, Play Mode, Profiler, GCMonitor, and player build are pending because no Unity MCP tools are exposed. Local static scans and dotnet build are evidence for source/assembly hygiene only, not runtime performance proof.
